<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">11262418</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2001</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>12</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2022</Year><Month>02</Month><Day>24</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">0021-9258</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>276</Volume><Issue>23</Issue><PubDate><Year>2001</Year><Month>Jun</Month><Day>08</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>The Journal of biological chemistry</Title><ISOAbbreviation>J Biol Chem</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Functional characterization of transforming growth factor beta signaling in Smad2- and Smad3-deficient fibroblasts.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>19945</StartPage><EndPage>19953</EndPage><MedlinePgn>19945-53</MedlinePgn></Pagination><Abstract><AbstractText>A prominent pathway of transforming growth factor (TGF)-beta signaling involves receptor-dependent phosphorylation of Smad2 and Smad3, which then translocate to the nucleus to activate transcription of target genes. To investigate the relative importance of these two Smad proteins in TGF-beta1 signal transduction, we have utilized a loss of function approach, based on analysis of the effects of TGF-beta1 on fibroblasts derived from mouse embryos deficient in Smad2 (S2KO) or Smad3 (S3KO). TGF-beta1 caused 50% inhibition of cellular proliferation in wild-type fibroblasts as assessed by [(3)H]thymidine incorporation, whereas the growth of S2KO or S3KO cells was only weakly inhibited by TGF-beta1. Lack of Smad2 or Smad3 expression did not affect TGF-beta1-induced fibronectin synthesis but resulted in markedly suppressed induction of plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 by TGF-beta1. Moreover, TGF-beta1-mediated induction of matrix metalloproteinase-2 was selectively dependent on Smad2, whereas induction of c-fos, Smad7, and TGF-beta1 autoinduction relied on expression of Smad3. Investigation of transcriptional activation of TGF-beta-sensitive reporter genes in the different fibroblasts showed that activation of the (Smad binding element)(4)-Lux reporter by TGF-beta1 was dependent on expression of Smad3, but not Smad2, whereas activation of the activin response element-Lux reporter was strongly suppressed in S2KO fibroblasts but, on the contrary, enhanced in S3KO cells. Our findings indicate specific roles for Smad2 and Smad3 in TGF-beta1 signaling.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Piek</LastName><ForeName>E</ForeName><Initials>E</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory of Cell Regulation and Carcinogenesis, National Cancer Institute, National Institutes of Health, Bethesda, Maryland 20892, USA. pieke@mail.nih.gov</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ju</LastName><ForeName>W J</ForeName><Initials>WJ</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Heyer</LastName><ForeName>J</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Escalante-Alcalde</LastName><ForeName>D</ForeName><Initials>D</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Stewart</LastName><ForeName>C L</ForeName><Initials>CL</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Weinstein</LastName><ForeName>M</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Deng</LastName><ForeName>C</ForeName><Initials>C</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kucherlapati</LastName><ForeName>R</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Bottinger</LastName><ForeName>E P</ForeName><Initials>EP</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Roberts</LastName><ForeName>A B</ForeName><Initials>AB</Initials></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>DK-56077-01</GrantID><Acronym>DK</Acronym><Agency>NIDDK NIH HHS</Agency><Country>United States</Country></Grant></GrantList><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType 